# C# Additional File Functions

## 1.  Other function using Reading Files

### File.ReadLines()
Reads a text file line by line. It returns each line as a string.

### StreamReader.ReadBlock()
Reads a specific number of characters from a file into a character array.

### FileStream.Read()
Reads bytes from a file into a byte array.

### File.ReadAllBytes()
Reads all the contents of a file as bytes. It is useful for binary files such as images and other files.

## 2. Deleting Files

### File.Delete()
Deletes a file using its file path.

### FileInfo.Delete()
Deletes a file using a FileInfo object.

### Directory.Delete()
Deletes a directory or folder. Using recursive: true deletes the folder and everything inside it.

Warning: Be careful when deleting files and folders because deleted data may not be recoverable.

## 3. Creating and Writing Files

### File.WriteAllText()
Creates a new text file and writes text into it. If the file already exists, its previous contents are replaced.

### File.AppendAllText()
Adds new text to the end of a file. If the file does not exist, it creates a new file.

## Summary

Reading functions are used to retrieve information from files.

Deleting functions are used to remove files and folders.

Creating and writing functions are used to create files and save text.

These are additional C# file functions that can be studied separately from the functions covered in the class notes.